L95: ## Glob: AbortSignal cancellation bypass allows post-timeout filesystem traversal
L96: 
L97: Moderate severity GitHub Reviewed Published Oct 27, 2026 in 【46†isaacs/node-glob】 • Updated Oct 30, 2026
L98: 
L99: 【47†Vulnerability details】 【48†Dependabot alerts 0】
L100: 
L101: ## Package
L102: 
L103: glob (【49†npm】)
L104: 
L105: ## Affected versions
L106: 
L107: >= 9.0.0, <= 14.0.1
L108: 
L109: ## Patched versions
L110: 
L111: 14.0.2
L112: 
L113: ## Description
L114: ### Summary
L115: 
L116: In glob 13.0.6, an aborted async walk rejects or emits an error but continues recursively reading directories in the background.
L117: 
L118: The root cause is that abort branches in src/walker.ts call cb() without returning. As a result, callers that rely on signal or AbortSignal.timeout() to bound glob traversal cost can still incur filesystem I/O and CPU work after the request has already been rejected.
L119: ### Details
L120: 
L121: This was reproduced on glob v13.0.6 (commit e80cb38ae60d6cbff9e75f39032a994858994d35).
L122: 
L123: The README documents that the signal option cancels the glob walk. However, in src/walker.ts, the following methods call cb() when this.signal?.aborted is true but do not return:
L124: 
L125:   * GlobUtil.walkCB()
L126:   * GlobUtil.walkCB2()
L127:   * GlobUtil.walkCBSync()
L128:   * GlobUtil.walkCB2Sync()
L129: 
L130: Example vulnerable pattern:
L131: 
L132: if (this.signal?.aborted) cb()
L133: Because execution continues after cb(), the walker still reaches processor.processPatterns(), readdirCB(), and recursive walkCB2() calls.
L134: 
L135: The issue was directly demonstrated against v13.0.6. A source-history review shows the same missing-return pattern is present in tagged releases from v9.0.0 through v13.0.6.
L136: 
L137: The issue affects both glob() and globStream().
L138: 
L139: Recommended fix:
L140: Change each abort branch to return immediately:
L141: 
L142: if (this.signal?.aborted) return cb()
L143: ### Proof of concept
L144: 
L145: Environment:
L146: 
L147:   * Linux x86_64
L148:   * Node.js v22.23.3
L149:   * npm 10.9.9
L150:   * glob 13.0.6
L151: 
L152: Reproduction steps:
L153: 
L154:   1. Install the affected package:
L155: npm init -y
L156: npm install glob@13.0.6
L157: 
L158:   2. Run the attached PoC:
L159: node poc/abort-continues-work.mjs
L160: 
L161: The PoC aborts after the first readdir() call, waits briefly, and counts how many more directory reads happen after the promise has already rejected.
L162: 
L163: Observed output on the vulnerable version:
L164: {
L165: "rejection": "Error: probe-abort",
L166: "readdirCalls": 1365,
L167: "readdirCallsAtReject": 5,
L168: "readdirCallsAfterReject": 1360
L169: }
L170: 
L171: Expected behavior:
L172: readdirCallsAfterReject should be 0.
L173: 
L174: A second PoC using AbortSignal.timeout() also reproduces the issue:
L175: 
L176: {
L177: "rejection": "TimeoutError: The operation was aborted due to timeout",
L178: "readdirCalls": 133,
L179: "readdirCallsAtReject": 1,
L180: "readdirCallsAfterReject": 132
L181: }
L182: 
L183: The same test against a patched build with return cb() in the four abort branches stops immediately:
L184: {
L185: "readdirCalls": 1,
L186: "readdirCallsAtReject": 1,
L187: "readdirCallsAfterReject": 0
L188: }
L189: ### Impact
L190: 
L191: This is an availability issue.
L192: 
L193: Applications that expose globbing over attacker-influenced patterns or attacker-controlled directory trees and use AbortSignal / AbortSignal.timeout() as a traversal budget can still perform significant filesystem I/O and CPU work after a request has already timed out or been canceled.
L194: 
L195: Repeated timed-out requests can stack background glob walks and degrade worker throughput or service availability.
L196: 
L197: No confidentiality or integrity impact was observed.
